\title{CAUSAL NEURAL SET FILTERING FOR ONLINE MULTI-TARGET TRACKING}

\name{Zhongdi Liu$^{1,*}$ \qquad Huangyu Dai$^{2,*}$}
\address{$^{1}$Hangzhou Applied Acoustics Research Institute, Hangzhou 310023, China\\
$^{2}$Independent Researcher, Hangzhou, China\\
$^{*}$Equal contribution.}

\begin{document}
\ninept
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
% Transformer-based multi-target tracking (MTT) enables data association and state estimation to be learned jointly, but MT3/Track-MT3-style trackers recover temporal context by repeatedly re-encoding measurement windows, incurring redundant computation and representing history as raw observations rather than a recursively propagated posterior. We propose CNSF, a neural set filter that encodes only current measurements while carrying past evidence in the posterior track set. CNSF combines exclusive Sinkhorn association, association-conditioned Kalman-shaped posteriors with moment matching, and recurrent Bernoulli lifecycle modeling with measurement-driven birth. These mechanisms respectively enforce exclusive measurement use, propagate association ambiguity, and maintain target existence through missed detections and birth--death transitions. On a held-out three-regime benchmark, CNSF reduces mean GOSPA/Pro-GOSPA/T-GOSPA over Track-MT3 by 19.3\%/20.5\%/55.2\%, with 55.9\% fewer parameters and 3.76$\times$ faster single-thread CPU inference, while also outperforming MHT, $\delta$-GLMB, and PMBM in mean GOSPA and T-GOSPA.

% Transformer-based multi-target tracking (MTT) enables data association and state estimation to be learned jointly, but MT3/Track-MT3-style trackers recover temporal context by repeatedly re-encoding measurement windows, incurring redundant computation and representing history as raw observations rather than a recursively propagated state. We propose Causal Neural Set Filtering (CNSF), a neural set filter that encodes only current measurements while carrying past evidence in a structured recursive track state. CNSF combines exclusive Sinkhorn association, association-conditioned Kalman-shaped updates with moment matching, and recurrent Bernoulli lifecycle modeling with measurement-driven birth. These mechanisms respectively enforce exclusive measurement use, propagate association ambiguity, and maintain target existence through missed detections and birth–death transitions. On a held-out three-regime simulated test set, CNSF reduces mean GOSPA and T-GOSPA over Track-MT3 by 19.3\% and 30.4\%, with 55.9\% fewer parameters and 3.76$\times$ faster single-thread CPU inference.

Transformer-based multi-target tracking (MTT) jointly learns data association and state estimation, but MT3/Track-MT3-style trackers repeatedly re-encode measurement windows, incurring redundant computation. We propose Causal Neural Set Filtering (CNSF)\footnote{\href{https://github.com/daihuangyu/CNSF}{Code: https://github.com/daihuangyu/CNSF}}, a neural set filter that encodes only current measurements while carrying past evidence in a structured recursive track state. CNSF combines exclusive Sinkhorn association, association-conditioned Kalman-shaped updates with moment matching, and recurrent Bernoulli lifecycle modeling with measurement-driven birth. These mechanisms impose soft one-to-one constraints, propagate association-induced state uncertainty, and support existence estimation under missed detections and birth--death transitions. On a held-out three-regime simulated test set, CNSF reduces mean GOSPA and T-GOSPA relative to Track-MT3 by 19.3\% and 30.4\%, with 55.9\% fewer parameters and a $3.76\times$ speedup in single-thread CPU inference.

\end{abstract}

\begin{keywords}
multi-target tracking, neural filtering, data association, set prediction, Sinkhorn
\end{keywords}

\section{Introduction}
% Multi-target tracking (MTT) recursively estimates a time-varying finite target set from measurements corrupted by clutter, misses, births, deaths, and uncertain measurement origin. Classical Bayesian trackers make this recursion explicit: Kalman filtering provides the predict--update backbone when associations are known \cite{kalman1960}, JPDA/MHT model ambiguous measurement origin \cite{fortmann1983jpda,reid1979mht}, and $\delta$-GLMB/PMBM extend the posterior to multi-object existence uncertainty \cite{vo2014glmb,garcia2018pmbm}. These methods offer clear probabilistic semantics, but retaining many global association hypotheses can be costly in dense scenes.

Multi-target tracking (MTT) recursively estimates a time-varying target set from measurements affected by clutter, missed detections, uncertain associations, and target birth and death. Classical Bayesian trackers address these uncertainties through explicit recursive state, association, and existence modeling \cite{fortmann1983jpda,reid1979mht,vo2014glmb,garcia2018pmbm}, providing principled probabilistic semantics but potentially incurring substantial complexity as association uncertainty grows.

% Neural set trackers offer a different route by learning data association and state estimation jointly. MT3 and Track-MT3 formulate point-measurement tracking as Transformer-based set prediction with temporal context recovered from measurement windows \cite{pinto2021mt3,chen2024trackmt3}. While effective, this design repeatedly re-encodes historical measurements, introducing redundant computation and leaving the propagated track state short of the posterior role used in recursive filtering.

Neural set trackers offer a different route by learning data association and state estimation jointly. The Multi-Target Tracking Transformer (MT3) and Track-MT3 formulate point-measurement tracking as Transformer-based set prediction with temporal context recovered from measurement windows~\cite{pinto2021mt3,chen2024trackmt3}. Track-MT3 additionally propagates learned query-level information across frames, but overlapping historical measurements are still repeatedly re-encoded. However, its propagated queries do not explicitly carry kinematic uncertainty and target existence within a structured filter state.

% To address this gap, we propose CNSF, a neural set filter that replaces window-conditioned set prediction with posterior-state recursion, encoding only current measurements while propagating past evidence through the posterior track set. CNSF integrates exclusive Sinkhorn association, association-conditioned Kalman-shaped posteriors with moment matching, and recurrent Bernoulli lifecycle modeling with measurement-driven birth to handle measurement competition, association ambiguity, and target existence within a single recursive filter. On a held-out three-regime benchmark, CNSF reduces mean GOSPA, Pro-GOSPA, and T-GOSPA by 19.3\%, 20.5\%, and 55.2\% over Track-MT3, while using 55.9\% fewer parameters and running 3.76$\times$ faster. It also outperforms MHT, $\delta$-GLMB, and PMBM in mean GOSPA and T-GOSPA.

To address this gap, we propose Causal Neural Set Filtering (CNSF),
which replaces window-conditioned set prediction with structured
track-state recursion: only current measurements are encoded, while
past evidence is propagated through the recursive track state. CNSF
uses exclusive Sinkhorn association, association-conditioned
Kalman-shaped updates with moment matching, and recurrent Bernoulli
lifecycle modeling with measurement-driven birth. These mechanisms
support the recursion under measurement competition, association
ambiguity, missed detections, and birth--death transitions. On a
held-out three-regime simulated test set, CNSF reduces mean GOSPA
and T-GOSPA by 19.3\% and 30.4\% over Track-MT3, while using 55.9\% fewer parameters and achieving a $3.76\times$ single-thread CPU speedup. It also achieves the lowest mean GOSPA and T-GOSPA among the evaluated methods.

% \section{Related Work}
% \textbf{Probabilistic and learning-aided tracking.} Classical MTT combines recursive state estimation with explicit association/existence uncertainty, from JPDA/MHT to labeled-RFS, $\delta$-GLMB, and PMBM \cite{fortmann1983jpda,reid1979mht,vo2013labeled,vo2014glmb,garcia2018pmbm}. Recent signal-processing work continues to study multi-target association and recursive estimation \cite{zhong2025tphd,zhao2026geometric}, while learning-aided methods replace uncertain components of a model-based recursion, including association \cite{liu2019deepda,yoon2019association,li2022airborne}, Bayesian-inspired inference \cite{wei2024bait}, joint association/filtering \cite{cui2025deepaf}, learned Kalman corrections \cite{ketashvili2025kalman,golan2026aikf}, and deep recursive memory \cite{wang2026rmmnet}. CNSF instead targets the full set-valued recursion, linking prediction, association, posterior uncertainty, birth, and existence in one causal filter.

% \textbf{Neural set tracking and temporal representation.} Attention and set-prediction models \cite{vaswani2017attention,lee2019settransformer,carion2020detr,zhu2021deformable} motivate learned-query tracking. MT3/Track-MT3 adapt this paradigm to point-measurement MTT \cite{pinto2021mt3,chen2024trackmt3}, and persistent-query mechanisms are also used in TrackFormer, MOTR, GTR, TransMOT, and MOTRv2 \cite{meinhardt2022trackformer,zeng2022motr,zhou2022gtr,ruppel2022transmot,zhang2023motrv2}. CNSF changes the temporal interface of neural set tracking: only $Z_t$ is newly encoded, while $\mathcal T_{t-1}$---kinematic mean/covariance, Bernoulli existence, and learned query/lifecycle states---is the complete temporal carrier. The propagated set state therefore unifies exclusive association, association-conditioned posterior uncertainty, and lifecycle recursion rather than using persistent queries only as temporal context. Hungarian matching is used only for training supervision.

% \section{Related Work}

% Classical MTT is built on recursive Bayesian estimation. JPDA/MHT address association ambiguity, while labeled-RFS methods such as $\delta$-GLMB and PMBM further model target existence, birth, and death \cite{fortmann1983jpda,reid1979mht,vo2013labeled,vo2014glmb,garcia2018pmbm}. These formulations provide principled probabilistic treatment of multi-target uncertainty, but their complexity can increase rapidly as association hypotheses grow. Recent studies combine recursive estimation with learned components, including association \cite{liu2019deepda,yoon2019association,li2022airborne}, Bayesian-inspired inference \cite{wei2024bait}, joint association and filtering \cite{cui2025deepaf}, Kalman correction \cite{ketashvili2025kalman,golan2026aikf}, and recursive memory \cite{wang2026rmmnet}, alongside continued work on model-based association and estimation \cite{zhong2025tphd,zhao2026geometric}.

% A parallel line of work formulates tracking as learned set prediction. Attention and set-prediction models \cite{vaswani2017attention,lee2019settransformer,carion2020detr,zhu2021deformable} provide the basis for learned-query tracking, with MT3/Track-MT3 extending this paradigm to point-measurement MTT \cite{pinto2021mt3,chen2024trackmt3}. Similar persistent-query designs have been explored in TrackFormer, MOTR, GTR, TransMOT, and MOTRv2 \cite{meinhardt2022trackformer,zeng2022motr,zhou2022gtr,ruppel2022transmot,zhang2023motrv2}. CNSF follows a different temporal formulation: the posterior track set itself is recursively propagated, while only the current measurement set $Z_t$ is newly encoded.

\section{Related Work}

Classical MTT is built on recursive Bayesian estimation. JPDA/MHT address association ambiguity, while RFS-based methods such as $\delta$-GLMB and PMBM model target existence, birth, and death \cite{fortmann1983jpda,reid1979mht,vo2013labeled,vo2014glmb,garcia2018pmbm}. Association marginalization or hypothesis management can become costly as ambiguity grows. Related advances address association and filtering \cite{liu2019deepda,li2022airborne,wei2024bait,cui2025deepaf,ketashvili2025kalman,golan2026aikf,wang2026rmmnet,zhong2025tphd,zhao2026geometric}, including learned Sinkhorn–Kalman coupling \cite{li2024learning}. CNSF integrates soft exclusive association and association-conditioned moment matching with recurrent existence modeling and measurement-driven birth in a structured recursive track state.

A parallel line formulates tracking as learned set prediction
\cite{vaswani2017attention,lee2019settransformer,carion2020detr}.
MT3/Track-MT3 extend this paradigm to point-measurement MTT
\cite{pinto2021mt3,chen2024trackmt3}, with related persistent-query
designs in TrackFormer, MOTR, GTR, TransMOT, and MOTRv2
\cite{meinhardt2022trackformer,zeng2022motr,zhou2022gtr,
ruppel2022transmot,zhang2023motrv2}. Track-MT3 propagates query-level information while re-encoding measurement windows; CNSF instead propagates a structured recursive track state and encodes only current measurements.

