\section{Causal Neural Set Filter}
% CNSF is a causal recursive set filter: only the current measurement set $Z_t$ is newly encoded at time $t$, while all earlier evidence is carried by the posterior track set $\mathcal T_{t-1}$. We write the learned recursion as
% \begin{equation}
% \mathcal F_\theta:(\mathcal T_{t-1},Z_t)\mapsto\mathcal T_t,
% \label{eq:recursion}
% \end{equation}
% which is instantiated below by neural--physical prediction, exclusive association with ambiguity propagation, and causal target-existence recursion.

The central design choice of CNSF is to make the structured track state, rather than a measurement window, the carrier of temporal information. At each time step, the current measurements $Z_t$ are encoded once and fused with the previous track state $\mathcal T_{t-1}$; the updated state $\mathcal T_t$ is then the only persistent state passed to the next step. This gives the recursive update
\begin{equation}
\mathcal F_\theta:(\mathcal T_{t-1},Z_t)\mapsto\mathcal T_t,
\label{eq:recursion}
\end{equation}
where prediction, association, state update, and target lifecycle
are coupled within a single neural set filter.

